    "taxonId": 278242,
    "summary": "A small tree native to Brazil, strawberry guava has oval leaves and thin-skinned fruit ranging from yellow to dark red or purple. Its juicy fruit and skin are edible, fresh or made into jam and juice, although removing the skin gives a sweeter flavour. It is highly invasive in many tropical regions.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "The fruit's skin and juicy interior are edible; it is eaten fresh or made into jam and juice, sometimes after removing the skin.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p3", "s1:p4", "s1:p8", "s1:p22"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p22"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 59979,
    "summary": "A herbaceous elder native to southern and central Europe and southwest Asia, dwarf elder forms large groups of upright stems from an extensive underground stem. It bears flat-topped clusters of white, occasionally pink, flowers and glossy black berries. All parts are toxic, especially the seeds, though cooking can neutralize the toxicity.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": "All parts are toxic, especially the seeds, but cooking can neutralize the toxicity; explicit human food use is not described.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p4"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p4"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 1471431,
    "summary": "A deciduous perennial groundcover native to parts of North Africa, the Caucasus and southern and central Europe, it has thin, grass-like leaves and small pale-purple flowers on slender green stems. It flowers around May and June.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
