  {
    "taxonId": 866483,
    "summary": "A sprawling, downy plant found across most of Europe east to Iran, parsley-piert grows on fields, paths and bare, often dry ground. Its tiny green flowers have no petals and sit inside leaf-like cups. The slightly acidic leaves are edible raw and were historically recommended for salads and winter pickles.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "The slightly acidic leaves are edible raw; a 17th-century herbalist recommended them for salads and as a winter pickle.",
